/// The smaller of two integers.
export fn min(value: Int, other: Int) -> Int {
  if value < other {
    value
  } else {
    other
  }
}

/// The larger of two integers.
export fn max(value: Int, other: Int) -> Int {
  if value > other {
    value
  } else {
    other
  }
}

/// This value, with its sign removed.
///
/// Unlike `min` and `max` beside it, this one can fail: the least `Int` has
/// no positive counterpart, and it traps there exactly as the builtin did.
/// It is the first fallible function in the standard library. A trap raised
/// here names its caller ([issue #258](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/258))
/// and, since ADR 0058, is blamed on it: the caller's line is the diagnostic's
/// primary location and this file's line is the context shown beside it.
export fn abs(value: Int) -> Int {
  if value < 0 {
    -value
  } else {
    value
  }
}

/// The `Int` `text` spells in decimal, or an `Error` saying it spells none.
///
/// A leading `+` or `-` is read and nothing else is: no digit separator, no
/// radix prefix, no whitespace at either end, and no digit that is not one of
/// the ten ASCII ones. So `1_000` — which a Cove *literal* may be written as
/// — is an `Err` here, because the separator is something the parser of source
/// knows and this does not; `0x10` is an `Err` because naming a radix in the
/// text is not naming one, which is what `Int.parseRadix` is for; and `１２`,
/// U+FF11 and U+FF12, is an `Err` because a reader seeing twelve there is
/// reading a script this does not read. Every one of those is
/// `tests/e2e/values_int_parse`, and every one of them is what
/// `str::parse::<i64>` answered before this body existed.
///
/// It was that call, reached through one `Inst::IntrinsicCall`. ADR 0064 moves
/// it here: what counts as a number is a policy over a representation and not
/// an operation of the machine, and `parse` is a method's name that would have
/// to be renamed the day the method was, which is Decision 2's test. What is
/// left underneath is `core.byteLength` — a header load — with one run load a
/// byte over it. A loop the native tier compiles, in place of a call it can
/// only cross back out of.
///
/// **`Int.parseRadix` did not come with it, and the order #454 planned is the
/// other way round.** That variant refuses a radix outside `2..=36` by
/// *raising*, and a Cove body has nothing to raise with — issue
/// [#461](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/461). This one never raises:
/// every failure it has is an `Err` value it builds. So `parse` is not
/// `parseRadix(text, 10)` yet; when #461 is decided it becomes exactly that,
/// and this body moves under it.
///
/// # The accumulator runs negative, and that is the whole of the design
///
/// Cove traps on arithmetic overflow, so `value = value * 10 + digit` does not
/// answer `Err` on text that names no `Int` — it **stops the program**, which
/// is not this operation's contract. And the least `Int` has a magnitude the
/// greatest has not, so a positive accumulator cannot hold
/// `-9223372036854775808` at all, however carefully it is guarded.
///
/// Both problems have one answer, and it is the one Rust's own `from_str`
/// uses: accumulate **downwards**. `value` is nought or below at every step,
/// every input's magnitude is representable on that side, and the sign is
/// applied once at the end — where a positive answer is a negation that is
/// checked, because `-least` is the one negation that does not fit.
///
/// # One bound check a digit, and why the exact case is nested under it
///
/// `leastTenth` is `least / 10`, which truncates toward zero, so it is
/// `-922337203685477580`: the last accumulator from which a multiply by ten
/// still fits. The whole of the check is `value <= leastTenth`, and in the
/// common case it is false and costs one comparison:
///
/// - `value > leastTenth` — so `value` is at least `leastTenth + 1`, so
///   `value * 10` is at least `-9223372036854775790`, so subtracting a digit
///   of at most nine leaves at least `-9223372036854775799`, which is above
///   `least`. **Neither operation can overflow and neither is checked**;
/// - `value < leastTenth` — the multiply alone would go past `least`, so the
///   text names no `Int` and the answer is `Err`;
/// - `value == leastTenth` — the multiply is exactly `-9223372036854775800`
///   and what is left is whether the digit fits under it. `leastDigit` is that
///   room, `leastTenth * 10 - least`, which is eight; it is *derived* rather
///   than written as an 8, because a transcribed constant is a thing to get
///   wrong and this one is the difference between `-9223372036854775808`
///   answering `Ok` and answering `Err`.
///
/// Two checks a digit — `value < leastTenth` before the multiply and
/// `scaled < least + digit` after it — is the obvious writing and was the
/// first one. It is **+12.3%**: 8,420,009 instructions against 7,500,009 on
/// 60,000 calls over a 7-digit, a 19-digit and a 1-digit input, which is 1.70
/// instructions a digit for a question the common case does not have to ask.
///
/// **A digit-count fast path was measured and refused, and the measurement is
/// why rather than the taste.** A run of eighteen digits or fewer cannot
/// overflow at all, so a body could test the length once and then run a second
/// loop with no bound check in it whatever. On a 7-digit input that is
/// 2,040,009 instructions against 2,120,009, which is **−3.8%** — the check
/// costs 0.57 instructions a digit, not the two a reader counts, because a
/// comparison whose branch is never taken is most of a comparison and none of
/// a branch. On the mixed 7-, 19- and 1-digit workload above it is
/// **7,500,009 against 7,500,009**: what the two short calls save, the length
/// test on all three and the 19-digit call's unchanged slow loop give back
/// exactly. Two loop bodies for one operation, for nought on any input longer
/// than a hand-written number — and the census at `652a15c` is **0 dynamic
/// calls in covefmt and 0 in cq**, so there is no program in this repository
/// for that complexity to be earned by either. PHILOSOPHY's "earn complexity
/// through use", on a body whose use is a command line.
///
/// # Nothing here allocates on the path that succeeds
///
/// [`refuseInt`] is a function of its own for `std.string.refuseRange`'s
/// reason: the interpolation that builds the message is the only allocation in
/// this operation, and a body that inlined it would carry it at each of the
/// six places it is reached. A text that is a number pays for none of them.
export fn parse(text: String) -> Result<Int, Error> {
  let end = core.byteLength(text)
  var at = 0
  var negative = false
  if end > 0 {
    let head = text.byteAt(0)
    if head == '-' {
      negative = true
      at = 1
    } else if head == '+' {
      at = 1
    }
  }
  if at >= end {
    return refuseInt(text)
  }
  let least = -9223372036854775807 - 1
  let leastTenth = least / 10
  let leastDigit = leastTenth * 10 - least
  var value = 0
  while at < end {
    let byte = text.byteAt(at)
    if byte < '0' {
      return refuseInt(text)
    }
    if byte > '9' {
      return refuseInt(text)
    }
    let digit = byte - '0'
    if value <= leastTenth {
      if value < leastTenth {
        return refuseInt(text)
      }
      if digit > leastDigit {
        return refuseInt(text)
      }
    }
    value = value * 10 - digit
    at += 1
  }
  if negative {
    return Ok(value)
  }
  if value == least {
    return refuseInt(text)
  }
  Ok(-value)
}

/// The `Err` that says `text` spells no `Int`, which [`parse`] has already
/// found that it does not.
///
/// One sentence for all six ways of not being a number, which is what the Rust
/// arm said too: `str::parse` distinguishes an empty string from an invalid
/// digit from an overflow in its `ParseIntError`, and the arm above it threw
/// all three away and wrote ``​`{text}` is not an Int``. That is kept exactly,
/// down to the backticks — a text that is itself a backtick makes three in a
/// row and nothing escapes any of them, which
/// `tests/e2e/values_int_parse`'s `tick.*` rows pin rather than leave to be
/// discovered.
fn refuseInt(text: String) -> Result<Int, Error> {
  Err(Error("`{text}` is not an Int"))
}

/// `text` read as an `Int` written in `radix`, or the `Err` that says it is
/// not one.
///
/// One optional `+` or `-`, then one or more digits, and nothing else: no
/// space, no separator, no `0x`. A digit is `0` through `9` and then a letter,
/// in either case, for ten upward, and every digit is below `radix`. That is
/// the notation this method has read since it existed — it was Rust's
/// `i64::from_str_radix` — and `tests/e2e/values_int_parse_radix` pinned every
/// edge of it on both backends before it moved here.
///
/// # A radix outside `2..=36` stops the run
///
/// A radix of 1 has no place value, 0 has no digits, and past 36 there are no
/// letters left to spell a digit with. None of those is text the data got
/// wrong, so none is an `Err`: the call is wrong, and the run stops the way it
/// stops for an empty `String.split` separator — through `core.refuse`
/// (ADR 0067), with the three sentences worded below. It is checked first,
/// before `text` is looked at, so a wrong radix is refused whatever the text.
///
/// # The loop is [`parse`]'s, with the radix where the ten was
///
/// The accumulator runs negative for [`parse`]'s reason — the least `Int` has
/// no positive counterpart — and the overflow test is the same two
/// comparisons against `least / radix` and the digit that is left, which
/// [`parse`] computes from a constant ten and this from the argument, once per
/// call. `least / radix` truncates toward zero, which for a negative quotient
/// is its ceiling: any `value` at or above it times `radix` is at or above
/// `least`, so the product is never formed where it would not fit.
export fn parseRadix(text: String, radix: Int) -> Result<Int, Error> {
  if radix < 2 {
    core.refuse(radixRefused(radix), radixRule(), radixHelp())
  }
  if radix > 36 {
    core.refuse(radixRefused(radix), radixRule(), radixHelp())
  }
  let end = core.byteLength(text)
  var at = 0
  var negative = false
  if end > 0 {
    let head = text.byteAt(0)
    if head == '-' {
      negative = true
      at = 1
    } else if head == '+' {
      at = 1
    }
  }
  if at >= end {
    return refuseDigits(text, radix)
  }
  let least = -9223372036854775807 - 1
  let leastPart = least / radix
  let leastDigit = leastPart * radix - least
  var value = 0
  while at < end {
    // The value of the byte as a digit, or 36 where it is none: above every
    // digit of every radix, so the one comparison below refuses a byte that is
    // not a digit and a digit that is too large alike. Each range is two
    // `if`s rather than one `&&`, for `std.string.sliceBytes`' reason — a
    // comparison that is all an `if` branches on is one instruction — and it
    // is written here rather than called, so that a digit costs no call.
    let byte = text.byteAt(at)
    var digit = 36
    if byte <= '9' {
      if byte >= '0' {
        digit = byte - '0'
      }
    } else if byte >= 'a' {
      if byte <= 'z' {
        digit = byte - 'a' + 10
      }
    } else if byte >= 'A' {
      if byte <= 'Z' {
        digit = byte - 'A' + 10
      }
    }
    if digit >= radix {
      return refuseDigits(text, radix)
    }
    if value <= leastPart {
      if value < leastPart {
        return refuseDigits(text, radix)
      }
      if digit > leastDigit {
        return refuseDigits(text, radix)
      }
    }
    value = value * radix - digit
    at += 1
  }
  if negative {
    return Ok(value)
  }
  if value == least {
    return refuseDigits(text, radix)
  }
  // A `return` rather than the body's last expression: an `Ok` in tail
  // position is built aside and copied into the answer, and
  // `crates/cove-cli/tests/copies.rs` counts that copy once in every program
  // that lowers this body, where this form has none.
  return Ok(-value)
}

/// The `Err` that says `text` spells no `Int` in `radix`: [`refuseInt`]'s
/// sentence with the radix it was read in.
fn refuseDigits(text: String, radix: Int) -> Result<Int, Error> {
  Err(Error("`{text}` is not an Int in radix {radix}"))
}

/// What [`parseRadix`] says of a radix it cannot read in.
///
/// The three sentences were `vm::intrinsics::operand`'s `radix` and `builtins`'
/// `radix_error`, two copies in Rust; they are these and nowhere else now. A
/// function of its own so that the refusal is one line of [`parseRadix`] and
/// the path that reads a number carries no interpolation.
fn radixRefused(radix: Int) -> String {
  "`Int.parseRadix` cannot read a number in radix `{radix}`"
}

/// The rule a radix is held to.
fn radixRule() -> String {
  "A radix is 2 through 36, which is as many digits as the ten numerals and the twenty-six letters afford."
}

/// What [`parseRadix`] suggests instead.
fn radixHelp() -> String {
  "pass a `radix` between 2 and 36, such as 16 for hexadecimal"
}

/// Appends the decimal text of `value` to `buffer`: what `"{value}"` shows.
///
/// Not exported, and nothing a program can name: the lowering calls it for
/// every `Int` piece of an interpolation, into the buffer that string is being
/// assembled in (#403). It replaced the Rust intrinsic `Int.renderInto`.
///
/// # One digit a push, most significant first
///
/// The buffer only appends, so the leading digit has to be known before any is
/// written: `power` is found as the largest power of ten the magnitude reaches,
/// and each digit is the quotient by it as it is divided back down to one.
/// Each digit is one byte append — ADR 0062's length read, ensure of one, byte
/// store at that length and commit of one, which both tiers run as one step and
/// answer in place when the buffer has room (the interpolation sized it for a
/// whole `Int`) — so a digit costs a division, a multiplication and a store,
/// and no call.
///
/// The append is written out rather than called as `std.stringbuilder`'s
/// `appendByteInto`, which is the same four statements: one standard-library
/// module cannot name another's private function. The byte is bound first, so
/// that its arithmetic is not inside the window.
///
/// # Nothing here can overflow
///
/// Everything happens on the non-positive side of zero, because every `Int`
/// has a non-positive counterpart and the least `Int` has none above zero. So
/// `rest` is `value` or its negation and never above zero, `power` is minus a
/// power of ten, and a digit is the quotient of two non-positive numbers:
///
/// - `power` is multiplied by ten only while `rest / 10 <= power`. `rest / 10`
///   truncates toward zero, so ten times it is at least `rest`, and so is ten
///   times `power`: the product is never formed where it would not fit;
/// - `digit * power` is at least `rest`, so the subtraction stays in range;
/// - no divisor is zero, and `power` is `-1` only for the last digit, when
///   `rest` is above `-10`, so no quotient overflows.
///
/// It must never interpolate an `Int` itself, which would call itself.
fn renderInto(value: Int, buffer: ByteBuffer) {
  var rest = value
  if value < 0 {
    let minus = '-'
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
    core.bytesStore(buffer, at, minus)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
  } else {
    rest = -value
  }
  let tenth = rest / 10
  var power = -1
  while tenth <= power {
    power = power * 10
  }
  while power < 0 {
    let digit = rest / power
    let byte = '0' + digit
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
    core.bytesStore(buffer, at, byte)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
    rest = rest - digit * power
    power = power / 10
  }
  // The answer, written where the call wants it. Left to the loop, it is a
  // `()` made aside and copied, which every expanded call site would keep.
  ()
}
